{"mode":"training","language":"en","altTextLanguage":"","title":"","instruction":{"english":"","german":""},"allowInstructionLineBreaks":false,"readingText":{"instruction":{"german":"","english":""},"content":"","configs":[]},"sequences":[{"instruction":{"german":"<p>Read the sentences in Figure it out. <\/p>","english":""},"configs":[],"staticConfigs":[],"parts":["<p><b>Figure it out<\/b><\/p><p><b>1<\/b> The volcano destroyed buildings.<\/p><p><b>2<\/b> Lava covered the roads.<\/p><p><b>3<\/b> The police closed the area.<\/p><p><b>4<\/b> Buildings were destroyed.<\/p><p><b>5<\/b> The roads were covered by lava.<\/p><p><b>6<\/b> The area was closed.<\/p>"],"globalTypesConfigs":[],"staticText":{"text":"<p><br><\/p>","backgroundColor":"lightgray","isSet":false}},{"instruction":{"english":"","german":"<p>Choose the correct options to complete the rules in the grammar box.<\/p>"},"parts":["<p><b>Grammar | The passive<\/b><\/p><p>Sentences can be active or passive.<\/p><p>\u2022 In active sentences we put the person or thing who does the action (the agent) <sup>1<\/sup> <dropdown id=\"dropdown_0\"><\/dropdown> the verb.<\/p><p>\u2022 When we do not know the agent of the action, we can use the passive. We also use the passive when we want to focus on the action.<\/p><p>\u2022 The passive is formed with <sup>2<\/sup> <dropdown id=\"dropdown_1\"><\/dropdown> + past participle. The <sup>3<\/sup> <dropdown id=\"dropdown_2\"><\/dropdown> passive is <em>am \/ is \/ are<\/em> + past participle. The <sup>4<\/sup> <dropdown id=\"dropdown_3\"><\/dropdown> passive is <em>was \/ were<\/em> + past participle.<\/p><p>\u2022 We often don\u2019t include the agent in passive sentences.<\/p><p>\u2022 When we include the agent in passive sentence, we put by <sup>5<\/sup> <dropdown id=\"dropdown_4\"><\/dropdown> the agent.<\/p><p><br><\/p>"],"configs":[{"additionalAnswers":[{"answer":"before","correct":true},{"answer":"after","correct":false}],"id":"dropdown_0","type":"dropdown","exerciseUr